  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I didn't open the dashboard or wait for the rule to run, so neither has been seen working yet.\n\n**Data:** The logs have a `timestamp` field plus `response` (the status code, stored as a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `clientip`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url` and `referer`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-2b147a37 Web logs overview\" has 5 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code\n- Bytes transferred over time\n- Top source countries (`geo.src`)\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2b147a37-web-logs-overview-dd977bb3b075.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-2b147a37 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every minute.\n- **Condition:** it fires when more than 10 documents with a `response` of 500\u2013599 arrive in a 5-minute window. It matches on `response.keyword` with a regexp, `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- **State:** the rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n- **Actions:** it has no action attached, so it will only record alerts in Kibana and won't notify anyone.",
